    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most Texas homeowners pay no capital gains tax on the sale of their home. Federal law lets you exclude up to $250,000 of profit, or $500,000 if you are married filing jointly, as long as you owned and lived in the house for at least two of the last five years. Texas has no state income tax, so the only possible tax is federal, and only on gain above the exclusion or gain that does not qualify.</p>
<p>Below is how the exclusion works, how to calculate your real gain, what happens if you sell early or rented the house, and what paperwork to expect at closing.</p>

<h2>The Section 121 exclusion: $250,000 or $500,000</h2>
<p>The home sale exclusion lives in Section 121 of the Internal Revenue Code. According to <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701">IRS Topic 701</a>, you may exclude up to $250,000 of gain from the sale of your main home, or up to $500,000 if you file a joint return with your spouse.</p>
<p>To qualify for the full exclusion, you must pass three tests:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ownership test:</strong> You owned the home for at least 24 months (two years) during the five years ending on the date of sale.</li>
<li><strong>Use test:</strong> You lived in the home as your main residence for at least 24 months during that same five-year period. The 24 months do not have to be continuous, and they do not have to overlap with the ownership period.</li>
<li><strong>Look-back test:</strong> You did not exclude gain from the sale of another home during the two years before this sale.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the $500,000 joint exclusion, only one spouse has to meet the ownership test, but both spouses must meet the use test, and neither can have used the exclusion in the prior two years. A surviving spouse who has not remarried can still claim up to $500,000 if the home is sold within two years of the spouse's death and the other requirements were met.</p>

<h3>Has anything changed for 2026?</h3>
<p>No. As of November 2026, the limits are still $250,000 and $500,000. They are not indexed for inflation and have not changed since the exclusion took effect in 1997. The 2025 federal tax law known as the One Big Beautiful Bill Act did not change Section 121. Bills to raise or eliminate the caps have been introduced in Congress, but none has been enacted. Plan around the current numbers, not proposals.</p>

<h2>Selling early: the partial exclusion</h2>
<p>If you sell before meeting the two-year tests, you may still qualify for a reduced exclusion when the main reason for the sale was one of these, according to <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p523">IRS Publication 523</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Work:</strong> A new job location at least 50 miles farther from the home than your old work location.</li>
<li><strong>Health:</strong> Moving to obtain, provide or facilitate care for an illness or injury, including when a doctor recommends a change of residence.</li>
<li><strong>Unforeseeable events:</strong> Examples include the home being destroyed or condemned, a casualty loss from a natural or man-made disaster, death, divorce or legal separation, becoming eligible for unemployment, or a multiple birth from the same pregnancy.</li>
</ul>
<p>The math is a fraction of 24 months (or 730 days). Take the shortest of: the time you lived in the home, the time you owned it, or the time since you last used the exclusion. Divide by 24 and multiply by $250,000 (per eligible person).</p>
<p>Example: A single Houston homeowner owns and lives in a house for 12 months, then takes a job in Dallas. Her maximum exclusion is 12 / 24 x $250,000 = $125,000. If her gain is under that, she owes nothing.</p>
<p>Hurricane damage can matter here. A home destroyed or condemned, or a casualty loss from a disaster, is on the IRS list of unforeseeable events.</p>

<h2>How to calculate your gain (and your basis)</h2>
<p>The exclusion applies to <em>gain</em>, not the sale price. Many sellers overestimate their gain because they forget what goes into basis. The basic formula is:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Amount realized</strong> = sale price minus selling expenses (such as real estate commissions, advertising and legal fees for the sale).</li>
<li><strong>Adjusted basis</strong> = purchase price plus certain settlement costs from when you bought, plus the cost of improvements, minus depreciation and certain other items such as casualty losses you deducted.</li>
<li><strong>Gain</strong> = amount realized minus adjusted basis.</li>
</ol>
<p>Settlement costs you can add to basis include items such as owner's title insurance, recording fees, survey fees, abstract fees, transfer taxes and legal fees for the title search or deed. Costs of getting your mortgage, like loan origination charges, and fire insurance premiums do not count.</p>
<p>Improvements are work that adds value, extends the home's useful life or adapts it to new uses: a new roof, an addition, a remodeled kitchen, foundation work, a new HVAC system. Routine repairs and maintenance, like patching drywall or repainting a room, do not count on their own. Repair-type work can count when it is part of a larger remodeling project.</p>
<p>Keep every receipt and closing statement from the day you buy. If you have owned the home for 20 years, receipts for that 2008 roof replacement could cut your taxable gain.</p>
