<h2>Worked example: married vs. single seller</h2>
<p>Here is how the numbers play out for a Houston-area home bought years ago. The figures are hypothetical, chosen only to show the method.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Line item (hypothetical)</th><th>Married, filing jointly</th><th>Single</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sale price</td><td>$900,000</td><td>$900,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Selling expenses (commissions, fees)</td><td>-$50,000</td><td>-$50,000</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Amount realized</strong></td><td><strong>$850,000</strong></td><td><strong>$850,000</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Original purchase price</td><td>$300,000</td><td>$300,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Settlement costs added to basis</td><td>$3,000</td><td>$3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Improvements (roof, kitchen, HVAC)</td><td>$45,000</td><td>$45,000</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Adjusted basis</strong></td><td><strong>$348,000</strong></td><td><strong>$348,000</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Gain</td><td>$502,000</td><td>$502,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Section 121 exclusion</td><td>-$500,000</td><td>-$250,000</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Taxable long-term gain</strong></td><td><strong>$2,000</strong></td><td><strong>$252,000</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Notice how much the improvements matter. Without the $45,000 in documented upgrades, the married couple's taxable gain would be $47,000 instead of $2,000. To see what you would actually walk away with after payoff and closing costs, try the <a href="/tools/seller-net-proceeds-calculator/">seller net proceeds calculator</a>, and review typical <a href="/blog/closing-costs-in-texas/">closing costs in Texas</a> for the selling-expense side.</p>

<h2>Tax rates on the taxable part</h2>
<p>If some of your gain is taxable, the rate depends on how long you owned the home and your total taxable income.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Short-term (owned one year or less):</strong> Taxed as ordinary income at your regular bracket.</li>
<li><strong>Long-term (owned more than one year):</strong> Taxed at 0%, 15% or 20%.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation recapture:</strong> Gain equal to depreciation you claimed (or could have claimed) is "unrecaptured Section 1250 gain," taxed at a maximum 25% rate.</li>
<li><strong>Net investment income tax:</strong> An extra 3.8% may apply to the taxable portion if your modified adjusted gross income exceeds $200,000 (single) or $250,000 (married filing jointly). Excluded gain is not subject to it.</li>
</ul>
<p>For tax year 2026, IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32 sets the long-term capital gains thresholds based on taxable income:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Filing status (2026)</th><th>0% rate up to</th><th>15% rate up to</th><th>20% rate above</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Single</td><td>$49,450</td><td>$545,500</td><td>$545,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Married filing jointly</td><td>$98,900</td><td>$613,700</td><td>$613,700</td></tr>
<tr><td>Head of household</td><td>$66,200</td><td>$579,600</td><td>$579,600</td></tr>
<tr><td>Married filing separately</td><td>$49,450</td><td>$306,850</td><td>$306,850</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Texas adds nothing.</strong> The Texas Constitution prohibits a state personal income tax, so there is no state capital gains tax on your home sale. Sellers moving to Texas from states like California should note that their old state may still tax a sale of property located there.</p>

<h2>Home offices, rentals and depreciation</h2>
<p>The exclusion gets more complicated if you ever used the home, or part of it, to earn income.</p>
<h3>Depreciation is never excluded</h3>
<p>If you rented out part of the home or claimed a home office, you cannot exclude gain equal to depreciation allowed or allowable after May 6, 1997. "Allowable" means the IRS counts depreciation you were entitled to take, even if you never claimed it. That amount is taxed at up to 25%.</p>
<h3>Nonqualified use</h3>
<p>Periods after 2008 when the home was not your main residence, such as years it was a rental before you moved in, are generally "nonqualified use." Gain allocated to those periods cannot be excluded. A key exception: time after you move out, within the five-year window, does not count as nonqualified use. That is why a homeowner who moves out and rents the house for up to three years can often still sell with the full exclusion, apart from depreciation recapture.</p>
<h3>When a 1031 exchange fits instead</h3>
<p>A 1031 exchange lets investors defer tax by rolling the proceeds of investment or business real estate into another like-kind property. Your personal residence does not qualify. A former home converted to a rental and held for investment may, and in some cases owners combine both provisions. Read our guide to the <a href="/blog/1031-exchange-texas/">1031 exchange in Texas</a> for the timelines and intermediary rules.</p>

<h2>Form 1099-S and reporting the sale</h2>
<p>In Texas, the title company handling your closing is usually the settlement agent responsible for filing Form 1099-S with the IRS. It does not have to file if you sign a written certification, under penalty of perjury, stating that:</p>
<ul>
<li>The home was your principal residence.</li>
<li>The sale price was $250,000 or less, or $500,000 or less if you are married.</li>
<li>There was no period of nonqualified use after 2008.</li>
<li>All of the gain is excludable under Section 121.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you cannot sign it, expect a 1099-S. If you receive one, you must report the sale on your tax return even when the entire gain is excluded. You also must report the sale if any part of the gain is taxable. Reporting happens on Form 8949 and Schedule D. If all your gain is excludable and you received no 1099-S, you generally do not need to report the sale.</p>
<p>Tax rules have exceptions this article cannot cover, so confirm your situation with a CPA or tax professional before you sell.</p>
[[product:document-scanner]]

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>For most Houston and Texas homeowners, capital gains tax on the sale of a home is zero: owning and living in the house for two of the last five years shields up to $250,000 of gain, or $500,000 for married couples, and Texas adds no state tax. The sellers who owe are those with very large gains, short ownership, rental use or depreciation. Track your basis carefully, keep improvement receipts, and run the numbers before you list. Browse more <a href="/category/selling/">selling guides</a> to plan the rest of your sale.</p>
